[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is a web security vulnerability that allows an attacker to induce users to perform actions that they do not intend to perform. It exploits the trust that a site has in a user's browser. While CSRF is not directly a JavaScript vulnerability, JavaScript code running in a user's browser is often the vehicle through which CSRF attacks are carried out."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Attack"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A CSRF attack occurs when a malicious website, email, blog, instant message, or program causes a user's web browser to perform an unwanted action on a trusted site when the user is authenticated. CSRF attacks target state-changing requests, not data theft, since the attacker cannot see the response to the forged request."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, imagine a user is logged into their bank account on 'bank.example.com'. A malicious website 'evil.com' contains HTML that triggers a request to 'bank.example.com/transfer?account=attacker&amount=1000'. If the user visits 'evil.com' while still logged into 'bank.example.com', their browser will automatically include the bank's session cookie with the request, effectively authorizing the transfer."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"How JavaScript is Involved"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript is often used to dynamically create and submit forms or make AJAX requests that trigger the unwanted actions. An attacker can embed JavaScript in a malicious website to construct and send these requests without the user's knowledge."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a CSRF attack using JavaScript
function performTransfer() {
  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
  xhr.open('POST', 'https://bank.example.com/transfer', true);
  xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded');
  xhr.withCredentials = true; // Ensure cookies are sent
  xhr.send('account=attacker&amount=1000');
}

// This function would be triggered when the user visits the malicious site
performTransfer();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Prevention Techniques"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synchronizer Token Pattern (CSRF Tokens): The server generates a unique, unpredictable token for each user session. This token is included in forms and AJAX requests. The server verifies the token on each request to ensure it matches the user's session. This is the most common and effective defense."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Example of using a CSRF token in a form
<form action="/transfer" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
  <input type="text" name="account" value="">
  <input type="text" name="amount" value="">
  <button type="submit">Transfer</button>
</form>"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Double Submit Cookie: The server sets a cookie with a random value. The same value is also included as a hidden field in forms. On submission, JavaScript reads the cookie value and includes it as a request parameter. The server verifies that the cookie value and the request parameter match."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Example of Double Submit Cookie in JavaScript
// Setting the cookie (server-side)
document.cookie = "csrf_token=random_value; path=/";

// Reading the cookie and adding it to the request (client-side)
function sendRequest() {
  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
  xhr.open('POST', '/transfer', true);
  xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded');
  const csrfToken = getCookie('csrf_token'); // Function to retrieve cookie value
  xhr.send('account=attacker&amount=1000&csrf_token=' + csrfToken);
}"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SameSite Cookie Attribute: The 'SameSite' attribute can be added to cookies to control when they are sent with cross-site requests. Setting it to 'Strict' or 'Lax' can mitigate CSRF attacks. 'Strict' provides the strongest protection but may affect legitimate cross-site navigation. 'Lax' is more lenient and allows cookies to be sent with top-level navigations."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example of setting SameSite cookie attribute (server-side)
Set-Cookie: sessionid=random_session_id; SameSite=Strict; Secure; HttpOnly"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Checking the 'Origin' and 'Referer' Headers: The server can check the 'Origin' and 'Referer' headers of incoming requests to verify that the request originated from the expected domain. However, these headers can be unreliable, so this method should be used with caution and not as the sole defense."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"User Interaction: Implement mechanisms that require user interaction for sensitive operations, such as CAPTCHAs or password re-entry."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use HTTPS to protect against man-in-the-middle attacks that could steal CSRF tokens."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement CSRF protection on all state-changing requests, not just those that appear sensitive."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly review and update your CSRF protection mechanisms to address new attack vectors."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding CSRF attacks and implementing appropriate prevention techniques, you can significantly reduce the risk of your web applications being exploited."}]
